  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">BEL: Rehabilitation of Degraded Peatland</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Belarus contains vast peatlands, covering 12.5% of its territory (2,560 thousand hectares), with over 480 thousand hectares urgently requiring restoration. A legislative framework guides ecological rehabilitation, prioritizing areas impacted by radioactive contamination from the Chernobyl disaster. Among 24 contaminated peatlands, the Kolybanskoe site (832 ha) in the Polesye Radioactive Nature Reserve is particularly vulnerable. Accelerated drying due to climate change increases fire risks, which are especially dangerous in radioactive zones. Fires can disperse radionuclide-laden ash up to 1,500 km, posing severe environmental and public health threats. 
Restoration of degraded peatlands in radionuclide affected area is therefore a critical priority for Belarus and  all the Europe. Rewetting through scientifically justified and specially designed, dams, dykes, regulating sluices  mitigates these risks while supporting biodiversity, carbon storage, and climate regulation. Restored wetlands also align with international commitments to remediate Chernobyl&#x2019;s legacy. 
Within the state programme, 380 hectares of Kolybanskoe&#x2019;s peatland, and particularly northern  past have been re-wetted, while 452 hectares in the southern part remain degraded. The proposed project aims to prepare scientific rationale for re-wetting southern part of Kolybanskoe peatland and prepare grounds for monitoring of outcomes.</narrative>
  </description>
